HST Office: digital signatures with USB tokens (PKCS#11) for Vietnamese CA users.

- Token certificates vanished after any PDF export in the session (Linux, macOS): comphelper
  (document hashes, package crypto) initialised NSS without a database when it ran first, so
  the signing code's later NSS_InitReadWrite() was a no-op. comphelper now initialises NSS
  through the NSSInitializer service (the same profile database as the signing code) and uses
  NSS_NoDB_Init() only without a component context. Loading the profile into a running
  database-less NSS was tried: tokens appear but NSS cannot map their keys to certificates, and
  NSS_Shutdown() fails (busy) inside soffice.
- Linux: also find the NSS database of the Snap (Ubuntu default) and Flatpak Firefox and
  Thunderbird, and fall back to the per-user shared NSS database ~/.pki/nssdb before a
  throw-away temporary one, so a token registered with modutil is found.
- The token PIN prompt said "The file “<last path segment>” is password-protected"; it now
  asks for the PIN of the named signing device, and a wrong PIN warns that the device may lock.
  NSS names the request "pkcs11:token=<name>" (RFC 7512 form); uui recognises that prefix.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/runtime/check-digital-signature.py (SoftHSM2 virtual token).
